1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:03,990
Imagine the world holding an awards
ceremony in nineteen

2
00:00:03,990 --> 00:00:07,820
forty-five. Britain has helped win the
war, but its finances are

3
00:00:07,820 --> 00:00:13,480
exhausted. The Soviet Union has gained
enormous power, but millions of its people

4
00:00:13,480 --> 00:00:13,960
are dead.

5
00:00:15,060 --> 00:00:16,420
Germany and Japan have surrendered.

6
00:00:17,220 --> 00:00:18,180
Their cities are devastated.

7
00:00:19,760 --> 00:00:21,740
Now move the camera forward a generation.

8
00:00:22,640 --> 00:00:25,480
The defeated countries are becoming
manufacturing giants.

9
00:00:26,660 --> 00:00:29,340
Canada has built a much larger industrial
base.

10
00:00:29,340 --> 00:00:33,600
Australia is attracting migrants and
building a different economy.

11
00:00:34,540 --> 00:00:38,240
And the United States sits at the center
of a new international order.

12
00:00:39,360 --> 00:00:42,840
So which countries gained the most after
World War Two?

13
00:00:42,840 --> 00:00:44,420
The obvious answer is America.

14
00:00:45,200 --> 00:00:48,560
But that answer becomes more interesting
when we ask what winning

15
00:00:48,560 --> 00:00:49,480
actually means.

16
00:00:50,280 --> 00:00:50,760
More factories?

17
00:00:51,640 --> 00:00:52,160
More influence?

18
00:00:52,940 --> 00:00:56,520
More territory? Or a better life for the
people who survived?

19
00:00:57,240 --> 00:01:01,520
To find out, we need three different
scorecards, and two different

20
00:01:01,520 --> 00:01:06,160
clocks. Because the country standing
tallest when the shooting stops is not

21
00:01:06,160 --> 00:01:10,520
necessarily the country with the most
remarkable story thirty years

22
00:01:10,520 --> 00:01:14,800
later. And a government gaining power is
not the same as its citizens

23
00:01:14,800 --> 00:01:15,600
gaining prosperity.

24
00:01:17,020 --> 00:01:20,700
The first scorecard measures economic
capacity: factories,

25
00:01:21,260 --> 00:01:25,180
infrastructure, trade, and the ability to
produce useful things.

26
00:01:25,180 --> 00:01:29,560
The second measures geopolitical power:
military reach,

27
00:01:30,120 --> 00:01:33,180
alliances, territory, and influence over
the rules.

28
00:01:34,080 --> 00:01:37,820
The third asks about recovery and living
standards over the following

29
00:01:37,820 --> 00:01:43,200
decades. Our first clock stops around the
end of the war and the early postwar

30
00:01:43,200 --> 00:01:48,020
years. The second runs through the
nineteen-fifties and sixties.

31
00:01:48,020 --> 00:01:52,320
We will follow six important cases, rather
than pretend there is one

32
00:01:52,320 --> 00:01:55,840
scientifically correct ranking of every
country on Earth.

33
00:01:56,980 --> 00:01:58,800
And one distinction matters throughout.

34
00:01:59,640 --> 00:02:03,880
Benefiting from the position created by a
war does not mean the war was

35
00:02:03,880 --> 00:02:04,840
good for a country.

36
00:02:05,680 --> 00:02:07,980
Rebuilding a ruined house can be
impressive.

37
00:02:08,780 --> 00:02:12,040
It does not mean burning the house down
was a sensible renovation

38
00:02:12,040 --> 00:02:15,460
strategy. Start with the United States.

39
00:02:16,040 --> 00:02:19,360
It was already a major industrial power
before the war.

40
00:02:19,360 --> 00:02:22,660
The conflict did not invent American
wealth.

41
00:02:23,420 --> 00:02:26,880
What it did was mobilize that existing
strength on an extraordinary

42
00:02:26,880 --> 00:02:31,240
scale, while much of the industrial
heartland of Europe and Asia

43
00:02:31,240 --> 00:02:32,960
suffered direct destruction.

44
00:02:34,100 --> 00:02:38,260
American workers and businesses learned to
manufacture ships, aircraft,

45
00:02:38,980 --> 00:02:41,200
vehicles, and equipment in huge
quantities.

46
00:02:42,220 --> 00:02:46,020
The National Park Service describes the
United States as the world's

47
00:02:46,020 --> 00:02:50,240
leading industrial producer by nineteen
forty-five.

48
00:02:50,240 --> 00:02:54,100
Its main continental industrial centers
had not been turned into

49
00:02:54,100 --> 00:02:58,120
battlefields or subjected to sustained
bombing campaigns.

50
00:02:59,140 --> 00:03:01,960
Picture two competing workshops when peace
arrives.

51
00:03:02,800 --> 00:03:04,640
One has trained workers, machines,

52
00:03:05,440 --> 00:03:06,560
electricity, and a roof.

53
00:03:07,300 --> 00:03:11,280
The other has talented workers too, but
first needs to repair the roof

54
00:03:11,280 --> 00:03:12,460
and reconnect the power.

55
00:03:13,180 --> 00:03:17,000
Both can recover. One can begin filling
orders much sooner.

56
00:03:18,180 --> 00:03:21,240
That is a relative advantage, and it
mattered enormously.

57
00:03:22,100 --> 00:03:25,800
Reconstruction created demand for
machinery, food, and materials.

58
00:03:26,840 --> 00:03:29,880
American productive capacity was well
placed to meet it.

59
00:03:30,580 --> 00:03:34,440
But converting a wartime economy to peace
still involved shortages,

60
00:03:35,220 --> 00:03:39,400
inflation, strikes, and difficult
decisions about what factories should

61
00:03:39,400 --> 00:03:40,000
make next.

62
00:03:40,980 --> 00:03:43,620
Nor was the American experience painless.

63
00:03:44,220 --> 00:03:48,700
Families lost servicemen. Wartime
discrimination and the incarceration

64
00:03:48,700 --> 00:03:52,920
of Japanese Americans exposed profound
failures at home.

65
00:03:53,700 --> 00:03:54,260
Calling America

66
00:03:55,080 --> 00:03:59,440
the clearest overall beneficiary describes
its relative economic and

67
00:03:59,440 --> 00:04:03,520
strategic position. It does not turn the
human cost into a profit

68
00:04:03,520 --> 00:04:08,460
statement. America's advantage extended
beyond the factory floor.

69
00:04:08,460 --> 00:04:13,020
In nineteen forty-four, delegates from
forty-four countries

70
00:04:13,020 --> 00:04:16,640
met at Bretton Woods to design a new
monetary framework.

71
00:04:17,480 --> 00:04:21,040
The institutions created from that effort
included the International

72
00:04:21,040 --> 00:04:25,360
Monetary Fund and the organization that
became the core of the World

73
00:04:25,360 --> 00:04:30,780
Bank. Under the system, participating
currencies were linked to the dollar

74
00:04:30,780 --> 00:04:35,180
through fixed but adjustable exchange
rates, while the dollar was tied

75
00:04:35,180 --> 00:04:37,720
to gold at thirty-five dollars an ounce.

76
00:04:38,440 --> 00:04:42,020
That arrangement placed the American
currency at the center of the

77
00:04:42,020 --> 00:04:46,580
postwar monetary system. It was not the
same system we use today.

78
00:04:47,660 --> 00:04:51,720
Then came reconstruction assistance,
including the Marshall Plan, and

79
00:04:51,720 --> 00:04:52,860
new security commitments.

80
00:04:53,720 --> 00:04:57,480
These helped rebuild partners while also
supporting American strategic

81
00:04:57,480 --> 00:04:58,120
interests.

82
00:04:58,860 --> 00:05:03,360
Aid was not simply money falling from the
sky, and leadership came with

83
00:05:03,360 --> 00:05:09,080
costs. Economic assistance, diplomacy, and
military commitments all required

84
00:05:09,080 --> 00:05:15,420
resources. Still, combine industrial
strength, financial influence, and an

85
00:05:15,420 --> 00:05:19,800
expanding network of partners, and the
overall verdict is hard to miss.

86
00:05:20,580 --> 00:05:23,920
America did not merely receive a larger
chair at the table.

87
00:05:23,920 --> 00:05:28,440
It helped design the table, the meeting
schedule, and some of the rules

88
00:05:28,440 --> 00:05:30,320
everyone would discuss around it.

89
00:05:31,280 --> 00:05:32,920
Now turn to the Soviet Union.

90
00:05:33,720 --> 00:05:36,740
On the geopolitical scorecard, its rise
was immense.

91
00:05:37,680 --> 00:05:41,340
The Red Army played a decisive role in
defeating Nazi Germany.

92
00:05:41,980 --> 00:05:46,380
At the end of the war, Soviet forces
occupied much of Eastern Europe,

93
00:05:46,880 --> 00:05:50,760
giving Stalin powerful leverage over the
region's political future.

94
00:05:51,600 --> 00:05:55,360
Over the following years, Soviet-backed
communist governments

95
00:05:55,360 --> 00:05:58,320
consolidated control across much of that
region.

96
00:05:59,140 --> 00:06:02,340
Moscow emerged as one of the two dominant
superpowers.

97
00:06:03,040 --> 00:06:06,980
For people living inside those countries,
however, greater Soviet

98
00:06:06,980 --> 00:06:10,980
influence frequently meant less freedom to
determine their own political

99
00:06:10,980 --> 00:06:15,240
direction. We also need to separate
influence from annexation.

100
00:06:16,320 --> 00:06:21,180
Poland, Hungary, and other states under
Soviet domination were not simply

101
00:06:21,180 --> 00:06:23,640
renamed provinces of the Soviet Union.

102
00:06:24,420 --> 00:06:28,500
Moscow could exercise extensive control
beyond its formal borders.

103
00:06:29,400 --> 00:06:33,220
Territory on a map and power over a
government are different measures.

104
00:06:34,460 --> 00:06:38,960
The Soviet state also retained or acquired
territory through the wartime

105
00:06:38,960 --> 00:06:40,200
and postwar settlements.

106
00:06:41,180 --> 00:06:45,320
Some territorial changes, including the
incorporation of the Baltic

107
00:06:45,320 --> 00:06:48,600
states, were not recognized by many
Western governments.

108
00:06:48,600 --> 00:06:53,220
This was a contested political order, not
a neat set of

109
00:06:53,220 --> 00:06:55,600
universally accepted border adjustments.

110
00:06:56,820 --> 00:07:01,120
But now put a second photograph beside
that map: an empty family table.

111
00:07:01,900 --> 00:07:05,540
The Soviet Union suffered the deaths of
tens of millions of people,

112
00:07:05,920 --> 00:07:07,100
military and civilian.

113
00:07:08,060 --> 00:07:10,080
Historians debate the exact total.

114
00:07:10,860 --> 00:07:14,780
No serious assessment can treat that
destruction as a small footnote

115
00:07:14,780 --> 00:07:17,440
underneath a larger sphere of influence.

116
00:07:18,600 --> 00:07:21,080
Large areas had been occupied and
devastated.

117
00:07:22,000 --> 00:07:23,100
Homes, farms,

118
00:07:24,160 --> 00:07:27,140
transport links, and productive capacity
needed reconstruction.

119
00:07:28,300 --> 00:07:31,820
The state emerged stronger relative to
many other powers while its

120
00:07:31,820 --> 00:07:33,880
population faced staggering loss.

121
00:07:34,720 --> 00:07:38,340
Those two statements can both be true at
exactly the same time.

122
00:07:38,900 --> 00:07:43,260
This is why calling the Soviet Union a
winner without naming the

123
00:07:43,260 --> 00:07:47,960
category is misleading. It was a
geopolitical superpower after the war.

124
00:07:48,680 --> 00:07:52,680
That does not establish that Soviet
households had received an economic

125
00:07:52,680 --> 00:07:57,440
windfall, or that territorial gains
compensated for the people

126
00:07:57,440 --> 00:07:58,620
who would never come home.

127
00:07:59,780 --> 00:08:03,620
Our scorecard therefore records a major
expansion of state power,

128
00:08:03,620 --> 00:08:07,040
alongside catastrophic human and material
costs.

129
00:08:07,780 --> 00:08:10,800
The point is not to cancel one column
against another.

130
00:08:10,800 --> 00:08:14,380
It is to notice that the columns describe
different things.

131
00:08:14,900 --> 00:08:19,000
Governments, economies, and families do
not share a single balance

132
00:08:19,000 --> 00:08:23,160
sheet. Canada offers a quieter but
important answer.

133
00:08:23,860 --> 00:08:27,900
It entered the war with substantial
resources and an industrial economy

134
00:08:27,900 --> 00:08:31,860
already in place. Wartime demand then
accelerated

135
00:08:31,860 --> 00:08:32,840
manufacturing,

136
00:08:33,640 --> 00:08:37,420
expanded production networks, and brought
new skills and technologies

137
00:08:37,420 --> 00:08:40,380
into factories supplying Canada and its
allies.

138
00:08:41,700 --> 00:08:45,860
The Canadian War Museum describes an
industrial transformation and a

139
00:08:45,860 --> 00:08:50,120
booming wartime economy, with full
employment by nineteen

140
00:08:50,120 --> 00:08:53,960
forty-two. Workers did not lose their
knowledge when the war ended.

141
00:08:54,740 --> 00:08:57,000
Machinery, organizational experience,

142
00:08:57,700 --> 00:09:01,740
and closer economic connections could
become foundations for civilian

143
00:09:01,740 --> 00:09:04,840
production, provided the transition was
managed.

144
00:09:05,940 --> 00:09:09,520
Canada also emerged with a more assertive
international role.

145
00:09:10,240 --> 00:09:14,020
It was a founding member of NATO in
nineteen forty-nine.

146
00:09:14,020 --> 00:09:17,300
Its postwar importance was not about
becoming another America.

147
00:09:17,900 --> 00:09:21,940
It was about a country with a relatively
small population gaining

148
00:09:21,940 --> 00:09:26,240
greater industrial capacity and a stronger
diplomatic voice.

149
00:09:27,320 --> 00:09:31,220
Again, this was not an untouched paradise
watching events from a safe

150
00:09:31,220 --> 00:09:34,320
balcony. Canadians fought and died
overseas,

151
00:09:35,360 --> 00:09:37,060
ships faced danger in the Atlantic,

152
00:09:37,940 --> 00:09:41,540
and Japanese Canadians experienced forced
displacement and

153
00:09:41,540 --> 00:09:44,740
dispossession. The industrial gains were
real.

154
00:09:45,300 --> 00:09:48,600
So were the sacrifices and injustices that
accompanied them.

155
00:09:49,560 --> 00:09:53,580
Australia's story is partly about what it
decided to build after the

156
00:09:53,580 --> 00:09:58,120
emergency. Wartime manufacturing had
expanded, and policymakers

157
00:09:58,120 --> 00:10:01,340
wanted to preserve and develop industrial
capacity.

158
00:10:02,140 --> 00:10:05,200
But a larger economy needed workers,
homes,

159
00:10:06,260 --> 00:10:09,600
electricity, and infrastructure. The end
of fighting did not

160
00:10:09,600 --> 00:10:11,680
automatically provide those things.

161
00:10:12,900 --> 00:10:16,460
The government established a Department of
Immigration in nineteen

162
00:10:16,460 --> 00:10:20,260
forty-five and pursued a major postwar
migration program.

163
00:10:20,940 --> 00:10:24,600
Migrants brought labor, skills, families,
and new communities.

164
00:10:25,500 --> 00:10:29,360
This helped reshape Australia over
decades, although discriminatory

165
00:10:29,360 --> 00:10:32,040
restrictions still limited who could
enter.

166
00:10:32,740 --> 00:10:34,900
Growth and exclusion existed together.

167
00:10:35,680 --> 00:10:39,580
The Snowy Mountains hydroelectric scheme
makes the story visible.

168
00:10:40,200 --> 00:10:43,300
Construction began in nineteen forty-nine.

169
00:10:43,300 --> 00:10:47,480
Workers from many countries built dams,
tunnels, and power stations.

170
00:10:48,320 --> 00:10:51,860
That was a postwar development choice:
turning investment and

171
00:10:51,860 --> 00:10:55,860
engineering into energy and water
infrastructure, rather than receiving

172
00:10:55,860 --> 00:10:58,540
prosperity as an automatic prize for
victory.

173
00:10:59,800 --> 00:11:02,840
Australia's strategic orientation changed
too.

174
00:11:03,530 --> 00:11:07,200
Britain's wartime vulnerability reinforced
the importance of American

175
00:11:07,200 --> 00:11:11,080
security ties, later formalized through
the ANZUS treaty.

176
00:11:11,920 --> 00:11:16,080
Australia gained opportunities from
industrial expansion, migration,

177
00:11:16,700 --> 00:11:17,260
and new partnerships.

178
00:11:18,380 --> 00:11:22,680
But those opportunities depended on policy
and effort over time, not

179
00:11:22,680 --> 00:11:24,560
merely on being on the winning side.

180
00:11:25,380 --> 00:11:28,640
At this point, the immediate answer looks
fairly clear.

181
00:11:28,640 --> 00:11:32,860
America leads the overall economic and
strategic story.

182
00:11:33,580 --> 00:11:37,360
The Soviet Union rises dramatically in
geopolitical power.

183
00:11:37,900 --> 00:11:41,440
Canada and Australia gain important
foundations for development.

184
00:11:42,400 --> 00:11:46,520
But our awards ceremony has a problem: the
clock is still stopping too

185
00:11:46,520 --> 00:11:50,080
early. Move it forward. Buildings return.

186
00:11:51,220 --> 00:11:51,500
Trade resumes.

187
00:11:52,640 --> 00:11:53,840
New institutions take shape.

188
00:11:53,840 --> 00:11:58,180
A country that looks hopeless in nineteen
forty-five can look very

189
00:11:58,180 --> 00:12:00,860
different in nineteen sixty-five.

190
00:12:00,860 --> 00:12:04,660
To understand that change, we have to
visit two defeated societies

191
00:12:04,660 --> 00:12:08,780
without confusing their later achievements
with the consequences of

192
00:12:08,780 --> 00:12:09,920
their wartime aggression.

193
00:12:10,820 --> 00:12:15,260
Germany and Japan did not receive a secret
economic bonus for losing.

194
00:12:16,200 --> 00:12:20,200
Their people endured defeat, destruction,
occupation, and profound

195
00:12:20,200 --> 00:12:20,660
upheaval.

196
00:12:21,380 --> 00:12:25,680
Their recovery happened afterward, through
a changing combination of

197
00:12:25,680 --> 00:12:26,480
domestic choices,

198
00:12:27,220 --> 00:12:29,680
international support, existing
capabilities,

199
00:12:30,680 --> 00:12:32,880
and the strategic demands of the Cold War.

200
00:12:33,980 --> 00:12:38,540
First, a precision that matters: we are
talking about West Germany, not

201
00:12:38,540 --> 00:12:42,200
pretending the two postwar German states
had identical histories.

202
00:12:43,280 --> 00:12:47,400
The western occupation zones became the
Federal Republic in nineteen

203
00:12:47,400 --> 00:12:51,820
forty-nine. East Germany followed a
different political and economic

204
00:12:51,820 --> 00:12:53,540
path under Soviet influence.

205
00:12:54,960 --> 00:12:58,620
In the western zones, the currency reform
of nineteen forty-eight

206
00:12:58,620 --> 00:13:00,940
introduced the Deutsche Mark.

207
00:13:00,940 --> 00:13:05,160
Changes to price controls and economic
organization helped money

208
00:13:05,160 --> 00:13:06,900
function more effectively again.

209
00:13:07,660 --> 00:13:09,540
A shopkeeper needs more than a building.

210
00:13:10,280 --> 00:13:14,740
Prices, supplies, and confidence in
payment all shape whether goods

211
00:13:14,740 --> 00:13:15,700
reach the counter.

212
00:13:16,540 --> 00:13:20,600
But one reform did not manufacture an
economic miracle overnight.

213
00:13:21,300 --> 00:13:24,120
Skilled labor, surviving industrial
capabilities,

214
00:13:24,820 --> 00:13:26,540
investment, and demand mattered.

215
00:13:27,200 --> 00:13:31,460
Marshall Plan assistance supported
recovery, including access to needed

216
00:13:31,460 --> 00:13:37,120
imports. European cooperation created
another route forward, with the coal and

217
00:13:37,120 --> 00:13:40,140
steel community and, later, the common
market.

218
00:13:40,940 --> 00:13:44,300
The result was a powerful industrial
recovery during the

219
00:13:44,300 --> 00:13:47,980
nineteen-fifties and sixties. Yet there
were difficult transitions,

220
00:13:48,820 --> 00:13:53,440
inequality, displacement, and people whose
savings had been badly damaged.

221
00:13:54,360 --> 00:13:58,640
A rising national output chart cannot tell
the whole story of every

222
00:13:58,640 --> 00:14:00,380
household standing beneath it.

223
00:14:01,340 --> 00:14:02,680
The important lesson is causal.

224
00:14:03,600 --> 00:14:06,960
West Germany's later prosperity does not
show that bombing cities

225
00:14:06,960 --> 00:14:07,940
creates wealth.

226
00:14:08,740 --> 00:14:10,980
Destruction was an obstacle to overcome.

227
00:14:11,660 --> 00:14:16,120
Recovery drew on institutions, people,
investment, and cooperation.

228
00:14:17,240 --> 00:14:20,700
We should credit the rebuilding without
treating the ruins as the source

229
00:14:20,700 --> 00:14:24,660
of the success. Japan presents a related
but distinct

230
00:14:24,660 --> 00:14:29,140
story. Defeat ended its empire and brought
an American-led

231
00:14:29,140 --> 00:14:30,280
Allied occupation.

232
00:14:31,320 --> 00:14:34,360
Political and economic reforms reshaped
the country.

233
00:14:35,060 --> 00:14:39,640
Japan had to rebuild an economy damaged by
war, restore production,

234
00:14:40,360 --> 00:14:42,980
and find a viable place in the
international system.

235
00:14:44,200 --> 00:14:48,140
The occupation's priorities changed as
Cold War tensions increased.

236
00:14:49,100 --> 00:14:53,240
American officials increasingly wanted a
stable, economically stronger

237
00:14:53,240 --> 00:14:54,320
partner in Asia.

238
00:14:55,100 --> 00:14:58,940
When the Korean War began in nineteen
fifty, procurement orders for

239
00:14:58,940 --> 00:15:02,600
supplies and services provided an
important boost to Japanese

240
00:15:02,600 --> 00:15:08,080
businesses. War elsewhere created demand,
with a terrible cost to Korea.

241
00:15:09,280 --> 00:15:12,860
That boost was not the entire explanation
for the following decades.

242
00:15:13,700 --> 00:15:18,260
Japanese workers, engineers, managers, and
households helped expand

243
00:15:18,260 --> 00:15:19,860
production and improve products.

244
00:15:20,600 --> 00:15:24,720
Investment, industrial capabilities,
export markets, and the

245
00:15:24,720 --> 00:15:26,840
international environment all mattered.

246
00:15:26,840 --> 00:15:31,340
An outside order can start a machine
running; it cannot substitute

247
00:15:31,340 --> 00:15:33,300
for everything that machine needs.

248
00:15:34,460 --> 00:15:39,180
By nineteen sixty-eight, Japan had become
the second-largest economy in

249
00:15:39,180 --> 00:15:43,100
the noncommunist world, according to
Japan's foreign ministry.

250
00:15:43,900 --> 00:15:48,260
The country we associate with cars and
electronics was emerging from a

251
00:15:48,260 --> 00:15:51,560
position that had looked disastrous only a
generation earlier.

252
00:15:51,560 --> 00:15:54,120
That is a remarkable transformation,

253
00:15:55,020 --> 00:15:59,060
not a retroactive victory in nineteen
forty-five.

254
00:15:59,060 --> 00:16:01,680
There is a moral and analytical boundary
here.

255
00:16:02,260 --> 00:16:06,660
Japan's later economic success does not
erase its wartime aggression

256
00:16:06,660 --> 00:16:09,460
or the suffering inflicted across Asia.

257
00:16:10,340 --> 00:16:13,580
Nor does that aggression erase the labor
of people who rebuilt

258
00:16:13,580 --> 00:16:14,180
afterward.

259
00:16:15,040 --> 00:16:19,240
Good history can hold both facts without
turning one into an excuse for

260
00:16:19,240 --> 00:16:19,600
the other.

261
00:16:20,460 --> 00:16:24,420
Before we announce a verdict, notice who
is missing from an industrial

262
00:16:24,420 --> 00:16:30,080
ranking. For people living under colonial
rule, the most important postwar gain

263
00:16:30,080 --> 00:16:31,080
might be sovereignty.

264
00:16:32,080 --> 00:16:35,580
The right to govern your own country is
not measured by the number of

265
00:16:35,580 --> 00:16:38,440
steel mills operating on the day the war
ends.

266
00:16:39,700 --> 00:16:43,040
The war weakened European empires and
changed the international

267
00:16:43,040 --> 00:16:47,320
environment. Long-running independence
movements seized opportunities,

268
00:16:47,320 --> 00:16:50,420
organized, negotiated, and fought.

269
00:16:51,220 --> 00:16:55,560
India and Pakistan became independent in
nineteen forty-seven.

270
00:16:55,560 --> 00:16:59,540
Across Asia and Africa, many other
societies pursued independence

271
00:16:59,540 --> 00:17:02,660
through very different, often violent,
paths.

272
00:17:03,740 --> 00:17:08,260
That freedom was not a generous wartime
gift from the great powers, and

273
00:17:08,260 --> 00:17:11,000
independence did not guarantee immediate
prosperity.

274
00:17:12,000 --> 00:17:14,540
Partition brought horrific violence and
displacement.

275
00:17:15,600 --> 00:17:17,380
Other struggles continued for years.

276
00:17:18,240 --> 00:17:22,640
A newly independent state belongs on a
sovereignty scorecard even when

277
00:17:22,640 --> 00:17:26,140
it does not lead our manufacturing or
military categories.

278
00:17:27,380 --> 00:17:29,300
Britain offers the opposite reminder.

279
00:17:30,140 --> 00:17:34,820
It was a victor with global influence, but
also financial strains and an

280
00:17:34,820 --> 00:17:36,680
empire under increasing pressure.

281
00:17:36,680 --> 00:17:40,620
Winning a war did not freeze the old
hierarchy in place.

282
00:17:41,360 --> 00:17:44,640
The postwar world redistributed
opportunities and constraints;

283
00:17:45,460 --> 00:17:48,400
different societies faced very different
starting conditions.

284
00:17:49,780 --> 00:17:52,960
So here is the verdict, with the
categories left visible.

285
00:17:53,720 --> 00:17:58,100
For the strongest overall position after
the war, the United States is

286
00:17:58,100 --> 00:17:59,780
the clearest answer in this comparison.

287
00:17:59,780 --> 00:18:01,540
Productive capacity,

288
00:18:02,280 --> 00:18:06,860
financial influence, and strategic reach
reinforced one another

289
00:18:06,860 --> 00:18:10,000
in a way no other country on our shortlist
could match.

290
00:18:11,300 --> 00:18:14,400
For geopolitical expansion despite
devastating losses,

291
00:18:15,160 --> 00:18:17,300
the Soviet Union is the essential case.

292
00:18:18,080 --> 00:18:22,220
For less spectacular but significant
industrial and developmental gains,

293
00:18:22,820 --> 00:18:25,140
Canada and Australia deserve attention.

294
00:18:25,140 --> 00:18:29,540
For extraordinary economic recovery over
the following decades,

295
00:18:30,160 --> 00:18:33,700
West Germany and Japan change the ending
of the story.

296
00:18:34,900 --> 00:18:37,900
And if the question is who gained the
right to choose their own future,

297
00:18:38,660 --> 00:18:41,320
the map expands far beyond these six
cases.

298
00:18:42,040 --> 00:18:45,700
There is no single podium that can fairly
combine a new currency,

299
00:18:46,520 --> 00:18:47,920
a larger sphere of influence,

300
00:18:48,780 --> 00:18:52,840
a factory job, and independence into one
universal score.

301
00:18:53,600 --> 00:18:57,430
The world did not hand out equal prizes in
nineteen

302
00:18:57,430 --> 00:19:00,200
forty-five. Some countries inherited
advantages.

303
00:19:01,260 --> 00:19:02,180
Some built new ones.

304
00:19:03,080 --> 00:19:05,620
Others carried losses that no later growth
could repay.

305
00:19:06,580 --> 00:19:09,840
The most revealing question is not simply
who won the war.

306
00:19:10,480 --> 00:19:13,500
It is who could turn the peace into
something better, and for whom.
